  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/61b89de34a6900d2ea39e5c2af141b3353e9ca790ac2748d0fc6d4d578127f93.png)\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$ (plotted above) is strictly increasing.",
  "worked_solution": "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x)$ using the product  rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+1>0.\n$$\n\nSolving $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x=-1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-1}\\,\u27f9\\,x=\\frac{1}{e}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that this critical point is a minimum. We also see that the slope of the tangent lines is negative to the left of the critical point and positive to the right of the critical point. \n\nSo, we summarize the sign of the derivative in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{aligned}x & (0,\\frac{1}{e}) & \\frac{1}{e} & (\\frac{1}{e},\\infty ) \\\\ f & \u2198 & \\text{min} & \u2197 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032} & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\\right|\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the interval $(\\frac{1}{e},\\infty ).$",
